We'll solve this problem in two steps. 

1st step: Parse the C# code for comments that start with "@@@".
2nd step: Turn these "@@@" comments into corresponding `Debug.Assert` statements.

This problem is basically a code parsing task, and it can be done using a variety of methods in C#. Here is a quick & simple solution for the problem using regular expressions:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string code = @"
void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
    bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
    // @@@ the template file exists
}";

        string pattern = @"\/\/\s*@@@\s*(.*)";
        
        foreach (Match match in Regex.Matches(code, pattern))
        {
            if (match.Success)
            {
                string comment = match.Groups[1].Value.Trim();
                string assertStatement = $"Debug.Assert({comment});";
                Console.WriteLine($"<code>{assertStatement}</code>");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In the code above, we've set a regular expression that matches any line of code that starts with "// @@@", followed by any characters until the end of the line. We then iterate over all matches, extract the comment (everything after "@@@ "), and print a code tag with the corresponding Assert statement.

Please note that this task assumes you want to replace the comments with Assert statements following the given format. Depending on your application's requirements, you might want to keep the original comments and only add the Assert statements, or vice versa.

Also, please have in mind that this code doesn't compile since the `Debug` class isn't defined in this context. You should run this code in a .NET environment, such as Visual Studio or a .NET Core/5 application. Also, this solution doesn't handle the cases where there are any syntax errors in the C# code provided. For a more thorough solution, you might want to use a dedicated C# parser, such as the one available in Roslyn.--------------------```cs
void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
}
```
In the code above, a Debug.Assert statement corresponds to the comment "@@@ the template file exists". This statement checks if the specified template file exists. If the file does not exist, the program will break into the debugger session because the assertion has failed.--------------------```cs
void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
}
```

The above code reads the C# code and converts the comment "@@@ the template file exists" into an Assert statement. The Debug.Assert method ensures that the condition enclosed in the parenthesis is true. If it is not, the program will break into the debugger.--------------------<code>
Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
</code>

