Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
</code>--------------------Based on the requirements, we can use Python programming language to solve this task. We will read the C# code line by line. If a line starts with "@@@", we will extract the condition (which is between "@@@" and the end of string) and output it as a debug assert statement:

```Python
import re

code = """
void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
}
"""

lines = code.strip().split('\n')

for line in lines:
    if line.strip().startswith('// @@@'):
        condition = re.search(r'// @@@ (.*)', line).group(1)
        print(f'Debug.Assert({condition});')
```
When this Python script is executed, it will output:
```
Debug.Assert(the template file exists);
```
This Python solution works for any code snippet that starts with "// @@@" and everything after "// @@@" until the end of line, and it will generate the corresponding Debug.Assert statement.--------------------```cs
void LoadTemplates(string fileName)
{
	bool templatesFileExist = File.Exists(fileName);
	// @@@ the template file exists
	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
}

```

In the provided C# code, an "@@@" comment was included about the existence of a template file. A Debug.Assert statement was generated, which is a basic form of assert in C#. It checks if the "templatesFileExist" variable is true; if it's not, it will throw an exception at runtime. This helps with debugging and verifying assumptions about program state.--------------------```cs
